<p>&#8220;Conservative and international students face different challenges: one feels forced to self-censor while the other reports fewer job opportunities. No significant impact on employment is found for either but conservative respondents report higher rates of satisfaction in philosophy.&#8221;<span id="more-57973"></span></p>
<p>That&#8217;s a brief summary of findings about conservative graduate students and international graduate students currently or recently enrolled in philosophy PhD programs, based on data collected by <a href="https://www.placementdata.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Academic Philosophy Data and Analysis</a> (APDA) in its <a href="https://philarchive.org/rec/JENMRF-3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2025 survey</a>.</p>
<p>In the following guest post, APDA founder and co-director <a href="https://faculty.ucmerced.edu/cjennings3/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carolyn Dicey Jennings</a> shares the details.</p>
[A version of this post also appears at the <a href="https://apda.ghost.io/2025-survey-results-international-and-conservative-students/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">APDA blog</a>.]
<hr />
